\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{An oriented link with nonreal entries}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On a three-site circle of spacing one, take the real flip
#{X} on spin and the two-dimensional fiber with its standard
orthonormal basis. Put the identity on both links other than
#{0\to1}, and on that link put the unitary
##{U=\begin{pmatrix}0&\ui\\1&0\end{pmatrix}.}
The field block in row #{0}, column #{1} of the centered
operator is #{X\otimes U^{-1}/2}. In row #{1}, column #{0}
it is #{-X\otimes U/2}. Thus in the transposed field matrix
the first of these positions has block #{-X\otimes U^T/2}.
The minus-branch spin matrix #{Z} negates #{X}, leaving the
block #{X\otimes U^T/2}.}

\p{Unitarity gives #{U^T=(\bar U)^{-1}}. Its lower-left entry
is #{\ui}, whereas the lower-left entry of #{U^{-1}} is
#{-\ui}. The transposed and spin-conjugated block therefore
belongs to the conjugate background in \ref{fdip-017R}.
For the Laplacian the two off-diagonal blocks before transpose
have fiber parts #{U^{-1}} and #{U}, with positive signs.
Their interchange gives the same conjugated link in the
Wilson combination.}
